WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.067 --> 00:00:02.334
-Good evening. I'm Seth Meyers.

00:00:02.334 --> 00:00:03.901
This is "Late Night."
We hope you're doing well.

00:00:03.901 --> 00:00:06.400
And now, if you don't mind,
we're going to get to the news.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:07.968
Today was Halloween.

00:00:07.968 --> 00:00:10.300
"Disagree," said Target.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:12.434
[ Laughter ]

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.067
That's right.
Today was Halloween.

00:00:16.067 --> 00:00:18.033
It's a great day
to ride the subway

00:00:18.033 --> 00:00:20.667
and try to figure out
who's covered in fake blood

00:00:20.667 --> 00:00:24.767
and who's just covered
in the regular kind.

00:00:24.767 --> 00:00:26.901
The White House last night
hosted neighborhood families

00:00:26.901 --> 00:00:28.133
for trick or treating.

00:00:28.133 --> 00:00:29.667
They didn't get
that many visitors, though,

00:00:29.667 --> 00:00:32.801
because kids know a raisin house
when they see one.

00:00:32.801 --> 00:00:34.467
[ Laughter ]

00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:35.901
That's right.
The White House last night

00:00:35.901 --> 00:00:38.000
hosted local children
for trick or treating,

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.434
and Biden got
a pretty serious scare

00:00:40.434 --> 00:00:42.100
when one of the kids
came dressed up

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:43.367
as his approval rating.

00:00:43.367 --> 00:00:46.167
[ Laughter ]

00:00:46.167 --> 00:00:48.901
"Aah!"

00:00:48.901 --> 00:00:50.200
The website --
[ Laughs ]

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:53.868
You didn't have to do
the double thumbs down.

00:00:53.868 --> 00:00:57.434
The website candystore.com
recently released

00:00:57.434 --> 00:00:59.601
a list of the most popular candy
in the country,

00:00:59.601 --> 00:01:01.968
including Sour Patch Kids
in New York,

00:01:01.968 --> 00:01:03.601
Butterfingers in Massachusetts,

00:01:03.601 --> 00:01:05.501
and Marlboro Reds in Florida.

00:01:05.501 --> 00:01:09.100
[ Laughter, applause ]

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:12.534
"Yeah, you can take two, kid."

00:01:12.534 --> 00:01:15.701
Former President Trump's sons,
Don Jr. and Eric,

00:01:15.701 --> 00:01:17.634
and his daughter Ivanka
are set to testify

00:01:17.634 --> 00:01:19.400
in Trump's civil fraud trial.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.267
And for the first time
in a long time,

00:01:21.267 --> 00:01:23.334
it's good to be Tiffany.

00:01:23.334 --> 00:01:26.467
[ Laughter ]

00:01:26.467 --> 00:01:29.033
The makers of Cup Noodles
announced last week

00:01:29.033 --> 00:01:31.334
that they will introduce
a new paper cup packaging

00:01:31.334 --> 00:01:33.367
that is microwavable,
and if that goes well,

00:01:33.367 --> 00:01:35.901
they're thinking about
introducing noodles.

00:01:35.901 --> 00:01:38.701
[ Laughter ]

00:01:38.701 --> 00:01:40.834
Rock bands Lynyrd Skynyrd
and ZZ Top

00:01:40.834 --> 00:01:42.601
yesterday announced
their upcoming tour,

00:01:42.601 --> 00:01:44.200
and if you're excited
about this news,

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:45.801
you're due for a colonoscopy.

00:01:45.801 --> 00:01:48.601
[ Laughter ]

00:01:48.601 --> 00:01:51.534
Firefighters in Michigan
recently rescued a raccoon

00:01:51.534 --> 00:01:54.734
that was stuck in a hole
at the bottom of a dumpster.

00:01:54.734 --> 00:01:57.300
Although, does that
really count as a rescue?

00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:59.400
That's like saying
you rescued Pitbull

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:02.534
from a VIP lounge in Miami.

00:02:02.534 --> 00:02:04.734
That raccoon was like,
"Aah! Leave me!"

00:02:06.968 --> 00:02:08.901
And finally,
the New Jersey Hall of Fame

00:02:08.901 --> 00:02:10.968
over the weekend
held its induction ceremony.

00:02:10.968 --> 00:02:13.501
So congratulations
to this tanning bed.

00:02:13.501 --> 00:02:15.033
[ Laughter ]

00:02:15.033 --> 00:02:16.300
And that was a monologue,
everybody.

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:18.734
We're off and running.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:18.734 --> 00:02:21.234
Got a fantastic show
for you tonight.

00:02:21.234 --> 00:02:24.067
She is a talented director
and an iconic actress

00:02:24.067 --> 00:02:26.367
who directs and stars
in "What Happens Later,"

00:02:26.367 --> 00:02:27.734
which is in theaters
this weekend.

00:02:27.734 --> 00:02:29.934
Meg Ryan is here tonight,
you guys!

00:02:29.934 --> 00:02:34.000
[ Cheers and applause ]
And she is a journalist and host

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:36.434
of CNN's "The Source
with Kaitlan Collins."

00:02:36.434 --> 00:02:38.567
Kaitlan Collins is joining us.

00:02:38.567 --> 00:02:39.901
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:39.901 --> 00:02:42.267
And we have
a very funny comedian

00:02:42.267 --> 00:02:44.534
whose memoir,
"Foolish: Tales of Assimilation,

00:02:44.534 --> 00:02:46.868
Determination, and Humiliation,"
is out now.

00:02:46.868 --> 00:02:49.734
Sarah Cooper
is at the show tonight.

00:02:49.734 --> 00:02:52.601
A reminder, during
the writers strike,

00:02:52.601 --> 00:02:54.400
it was a long five months off.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:56.501
We were very sad to be off.

00:02:56.501 --> 00:02:58.434
We're very happy to be back,
but one of the things

00:02:58.434 --> 00:03:00.501
I did during the writers
strike that I was happy with

00:03:00.501 --> 00:03:02.300
is I started a podcast
with my brother called

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:03.667
"Family Trips
with the Meyers Brothers."

00:03:03.667 --> 00:03:05.968
This is us
when we were last year,

00:03:05.968 --> 00:03:09.267
and we -- we have interviewed
a lot of guests.

00:03:09.267 --> 00:03:12.334
I'm going to show you
three of these are guests.

00:03:12.334 --> 00:03:14.033
Two of them were real guests,

00:03:14.033 --> 00:03:16.767
and one of them
hasn't been a guest yet.

00:03:16.767 --> 00:03:19.968
You got to guess
which ones are real,

00:03:19.968 --> 00:03:22.601
and then you have to guess
which one isn't.

00:03:22.601 --> 00:03:27.501
Okay?
Alright. Reggie Watts.

00:03:27.501 --> 00:03:29.367
John Oliver.

00:03:29.367 --> 00:03:30.501
Rudy Giuliani.

00:03:30.501 --> 00:03:34.267
Now...
[ Laughter ]

00:03:34.267 --> 00:03:38.767
I'm not going to give it --
I'm not saying anything.

00:03:38.767 --> 00:03:40.767
Also...
[ Chuckles ]

00:03:40.767 --> 00:03:44.167
"I remember we used to pack in
the back of my station wagon."

00:03:44.167 --> 00:03:47.100
Aww, such a good story.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:48.334
Also, I'm going back
to New Hampshire

00:03:48.334 --> 00:03:50.434
this Saturday night
at the SNHU Arena,

00:03:50.434 --> 00:03:51.501
Manchester, New Hampshire.

00:03:51.501 --> 00:03:53.267
That's where I went
to high school.

00:03:53.267 --> 00:03:54.300
It's going to be a lot of fun.

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:55.634
If you want to go to a show

00:03:55.634 --> 00:03:57.267
where my parents
are in the audience...

00:03:57.267 --> 00:03:58.767
[ Laughter ]

00:03:58.767 --> 00:04:00.300
...that's the one to go to.

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:03.701
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:03.701 --> 00:04:06.934
If you have been following
the news recently,

00:04:06.934 --> 00:04:08.934
you may have heard that "Barbie"

00:04:08.934 --> 00:04:11.334
has been making a killing
at the box office.

00:04:11.334 --> 00:04:17.434
A movie about a doll
made over $1 billion.

00:04:17.434 --> 00:04:19.734
If you've been following
the news recently...

00:04:19.734 --> 00:04:20.767
[ Light laughter ]

00:04:22.634 --> 00:04:25.667
[ Laughter ]

00:04:25.667 --> 00:04:27.634
We write this sketch a lot,

00:04:27.634 --> 00:04:28.734
and one of the things
we try to do

00:04:28.734 --> 00:04:31.734
is put a recent story
at the top,

00:04:31.734 --> 00:04:35.234
a recent story like "Barbie."

00:04:35.234 --> 00:04:37.033
You know, hearing things like
this always get me to thinking,

00:04:37.033 --> 00:04:39.567
I'm getting older
and sometimes I look around

00:04:39.567 --> 00:04:43.834
and I don't even realize
the world I'm living in anymore.

00:04:43.834 --> 00:04:47.334
Things are changing every day,
and not always for the better.

00:04:47.334 --> 00:04:50.567
So it's time to take a moment
to talk about how things were.

00:04:50.567 --> 00:04:52.868
Well, they were a bit
more simple...

00:04:52.868 --> 00:04:54.100
back in my day.

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:56.801
♪♪

00:04:56.801 --> 00:04:58.868
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:58.868 --> 00:05:04.100
♪♪

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:06.267
Back in my day,
a doll named Barbie

00:05:06.267 --> 00:05:08.067
wasn't killing it
at the box office.

00:05:08.067 --> 00:05:09.334
Poppycock!

00:05:09.334 --> 00:05:11.434
Back in my day,
a doll named Chucky

00:05:11.434 --> 00:05:13.968
was killing it on the screen.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:13.968 --> 00:05:16.367
And Chucky didn't have
some complicated relationship

00:05:16.367 --> 00:05:17.834
with a guy named Ken.

00:05:17.834 --> 00:05:20.467
What Chucky loved
wasn't complicated at all.

00:05:20.467 --> 00:05:22.834
It was the simplest thing
in the world.

00:05:22.834 --> 00:05:24.367
Murder.

00:05:24.367 --> 00:05:27.067
Stone-cold murder.

00:05:27.067 --> 00:05:29.634
He liked taking his knife and
stabbing people in the heart.

00:05:29.634 --> 00:05:30.934
[ Dog barks ]

00:05:30.934 --> 00:05:32.868
You're right!

00:05:32.868 --> 00:05:35.534
I guess that is what Barbie
does to Ken in that movie.

00:05:35.534 --> 00:05:37.334
[ Dog barks ]
What's that?

00:05:37.334 --> 00:05:40.767
[bleep] the patriarchy?
Alright.

00:05:40.767 --> 00:05:42.901
If you say so, Rusty.

00:05:42.901 --> 00:05:44.467
Back in my day,

00:05:44.467 --> 00:05:47.000
Jada Pinkett Smith
wasn't releasin' a memoir

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:49.601
unveiling bombshells
about her private life.

00:05:49.601 --> 00:05:51.267
No, back then,
Jada Pinkett Smith

00:05:51.267 --> 00:05:54.267
didn't have what you
and I would call a private life

00:05:54.267 --> 00:05:58.000
because of a little web series
called "Red Table Talk."

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:00.467
Whoever had that idea
must have had a screw loose.

00:06:00.467 --> 00:06:02.601
[ Dog barks ]
It was her idea?

00:06:02.601 --> 00:06:03.434
[ Dog barks ]

00:06:03.434 --> 00:06:05.601
Well, I stand by what I said.

00:06:05.601 --> 00:06:07.467
[ Laughter ]

00:06:07.467 --> 00:06:10.167
Back in my day,
there were no bedbugs in Paris.

00:06:10.167 --> 00:06:11.534
[ Speaking in French ]

00:06:13.067 --> 00:06:15.133
[ Laughter ]

00:06:15.133 --> 00:06:17.634
Back in my day, the only thing
in your bed in Paris

00:06:17.634 --> 00:06:19.033
was your mistress...

00:06:19.033 --> 00:06:21.501
until her husband found out,

00:06:21.501 --> 00:06:25.000
and then you found out that
his mistress was your wife...

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:27.133
and the four of you have a good,
long laugh about it

00:06:27.133 --> 00:06:28.634
while you drank
four bottles of wine

00:06:28.634 --> 00:06:30.000
on your three-hour
lunch break --

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:32.467
a lunch break you earned
during a six-month-long strike.

00:06:32.467 --> 00:06:34.968
And that is all the stereotypes
I know about France.

00:06:34.968 --> 00:06:37.300
[ Dog barks ]
Oh, and I suppose

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:39.000
you smell like a bed of flowers.

00:06:41.234 --> 00:06:43.200
[ Laughter ]

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:45.634
Back in my day, we weren't
spending $1,200

00:06:45.634 --> 00:06:47.901
for a new iPhone.
Applesauce!

00:06:47.901 --> 00:06:51.734
Back in my day,
we only spent $900 on a phone.

00:06:51.734 --> 00:06:53.234
And as we paid for it, we said,

00:06:53.234 --> 00:06:55.267
"I promise
this is the last phone

00:06:55.267 --> 00:06:57.334
I'm ever going to buy from you."

00:06:57.334 --> 00:06:58.734
And we stood by that promise

00:06:58.734 --> 00:07:01.767
until the day
the new one came out.

00:07:01.767 --> 00:07:03.634
And it was made of titanium.

00:07:03.634 --> 00:07:05.133
And I don't know what's better

00:07:05.133 --> 00:07:06.934
about a phone
made out of titanium.

00:07:06.934 --> 00:07:09.167
I just know that
whatever material my phone

00:07:09.167 --> 00:07:11.334
is made out of now,
I [bleep] hate!

00:07:11.334 --> 00:07:13.501
[ Laughter ]

00:07:13.501 --> 00:07:16.934
Get that weak-ass, non-titanium
[bleep] out of here!

00:07:19.501 --> 00:07:21.667
Back in my day, parents weren't
bringing their kids

00:07:21.667 --> 00:07:23.334
to trunk or treats on Halloween,

00:07:23.334 --> 00:07:25.434
where they got candy
from the back of a parked car.

00:07:25.434 --> 00:07:28.367
Back in my day,
if you were giving candy to kids

00:07:28.367 --> 00:07:30.267
from out of the back
of your station wagon,

00:07:30.267 --> 00:07:33.801
you'd face consequences
in your community...

00:07:33.801 --> 00:07:35.667
like not being invited
to the block party

00:07:35.667 --> 00:07:39.067
or losing your deal
as the spokesman for Subway.

00:07:39.067 --> 00:07:41.400
[ Laughter, audience groans ]

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:45.501
♪♪

00:07:45.501 --> 00:07:47.534
You told me it wasn't too soon.

00:07:47.534 --> 00:07:49.634
[ Laughter ]

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:52.234
Back in my --
Is that because...?

00:07:52.234 --> 00:07:55.200
[ Chuckles ]

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:56.667
Oh. Did you do it in dog years?

00:07:56.667 --> 00:07:59.234
Yeah, in dog years it's not.

00:07:59.234 --> 00:08:02.501
We -- You have to clarify
when we have a conversation...

00:08:03.534 --> 00:08:06.100
Well, 'cause I'm the host
of the show,

00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:07.901
so it's in my years.

00:08:07.901 --> 00:08:10.834
[ Laughter ]

00:08:10.834 --> 00:08:13.834
Back in my day, we didn't have
digital doorbell cameras

00:08:13.834 --> 00:08:16.400
so people could leave out
a bowl of candy on Halloween

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:17.868
and see who's coming
to their door.

00:08:17.868 --> 00:08:19.434
Raisinets!

00:08:19.434 --> 00:08:21.467
Back in my day,
there was a bowl of candy

00:08:21.467 --> 00:08:24.200
on a porch with a little sign
that said, "Please take one."

00:08:24.200 --> 00:08:28.000
You'd do a little look around,
make sure the coast was clear,

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:31.634
and dump that whole damn bowl
right into your backpack.

00:08:31.634 --> 00:08:34.834
And on the way out, you'd pass
a young father with a toddler

00:08:34.834 --> 00:08:37.467
and a Paw Patrol costume
and you'd say, "Don't bother.

00:08:37.467 --> 00:08:40.567
That jerk didn't
leave out any candy."

00:08:40.567 --> 00:08:44.601
And the father would say,
"Yes, I did. This is my house."

00:08:44.601 --> 00:08:47.200
But he couldn't catch you
because you was dressed as Ken

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:49.067
and you was wearing
rollerblades.

00:08:49.067 --> 00:08:50.334
[ Laughter ]

00:08:50.334 --> 00:08:52.567
♪♪

00:08:52.567 --> 00:08:54.467
And, yes, that was today.

00:08:55.834 --> 00:08:57.834
Oh, now?

00:08:57.834 --> 00:08:59.801
Now you got nothing to say?

00:08:59.801 --> 00:09:02.200
By the way, Rusty is here today

00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:04.534
courtesy of a waiver
from WAG-AFTRA.

00:09:04.534 --> 00:09:06.367
[ Dog growling ]

00:09:06.367 --> 00:09:10.200
Oh, you love that joke.

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:11.734
Back in my day,
we worried about --

00:09:11.734 --> 00:09:14.434
We weren't worried, excuse me,
about AI bringing down humanity.

00:09:14.434 --> 00:09:15.734
No way.

00:09:15.734 --> 00:09:18.501
We were worried about
Y2K bringing down humanity.

00:09:18.501 --> 00:09:20.634
We were worried the world
was going to shut down

00:09:20.634 --> 00:09:22.701
at midnight of the year 2000.

00:09:22.701 --> 00:09:25.467
And seeing what the past
23 years have been like,

00:09:25.467 --> 00:09:27.434
maybe it did.
[ Laughter ]

00:09:27.434 --> 00:09:29.200
Y2K, why not 2K?

00:09:31.801 --> 00:09:33.834
[ Laughter ]

00:09:36.634 --> 00:09:39.400
Back in my day, people weren't
accusing a chess champion

00:09:39.400 --> 00:09:42.801
of using vibrating anal beads
to cheat at chess.

00:09:42.801 --> 00:09:44.234
[ Laughter ]
It's a true story.

00:09:44.234 --> 00:09:46.367
Google it -- at home,
not at your work desk.

00:09:46.367 --> 00:09:49.300
♪♪

00:09:49.300 --> 00:09:51.634
Well, you see, back in my day,
they were accusing someone else

00:09:51.634 --> 00:09:53.200
of this particular method
of cheating.

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:57.434
But let me tell you, I won that
spelling bee fair and square.

00:09:57.434 --> 00:09:59.734
Now, you may have some follow up
questions about that story,

00:09:59.734 --> 00:10:01.467
so check it out on
"Back in My Day Extras"

00:10:01.467 --> 00:10:03.734
streaming on Peacock.

00:10:03.734 --> 00:10:05.701
Back in my day,
Twitter wasn't known as X.

00:10:05.701 --> 00:10:07.033
No, sir.
Back in my day,

00:10:07.033 --> 00:10:09.267
if you were typing
"X" in the URL bar,

00:10:09.267 --> 00:10:11.901
you were almost certainly
going to a porn website

00:10:11.901 --> 00:10:13.701
filled with nasty,
disgusting stuff.

00:10:13.701 --> 00:10:16.367
Not quite as disgusting
as what you can find on X today,

00:10:16.367 --> 00:10:17.334
but pretty close.

00:10:17.334 --> 00:10:19.367
[ Laughter ]

00:10:19.367 --> 00:10:21.467
Back in my day, we weren't
getting a "Frasier" reboot.

00:10:21.467 --> 00:10:23.601
Back in my day,
if you wanted to watch a sitcom

00:10:23.601 --> 00:10:26.167
starring a funny white man,
you just turned on "Seinfeld"

00:10:26.167 --> 00:10:27.267
or "Full House"
or "King of Queens"

00:10:27.267 --> 00:10:28.901
or "NewsRadio"
or "Spin City" or "Wings"

00:10:28.901 --> 00:10:30.701
or "The Drew Carey Show."

00:10:30.701 --> 00:10:32.267
And if you didn't,
you turned on "Martin,"

00:10:32.267 --> 00:10:34.133
which you probably should
have done to begin with.

00:10:34.133 --> 00:10:35.467
[ Dog barks ]

00:10:35.467 --> 00:10:37.400
"Damn, Gina" indeed.

00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:38.601
Back in my day,

00:10:38.601 --> 00:10:40.767
we weren't giving
former presidents gag orders.

00:10:40.767 --> 00:10:42.400
Hocus POTUS!

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:44.501
Back in my day,
we were giving former presidents

00:10:44.501 --> 00:10:46.133
a podcast
with Bruce Springsteen.

00:10:46.133 --> 00:10:49.300
And let me tell you...
well, I meant to listen to it.

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:51.334
[ Laughter ]

00:10:51.334 --> 00:10:53.634
Sure sounded good on paper.

00:10:53.634 --> 00:10:56.000
Well, I'm sorry
I had to do that, everybody,

00:10:56.000 --> 00:10:57.801
but sometimes
this grumpy old gramps

00:10:57.801 --> 00:11:00.834
has got a couple of ghosts
and ghouls to gripe about.

00:11:00.834 --> 00:11:02.300
This has been "Back in My Day."

